<body>
<div class="app">
  <div class="backstrip"><a href="spanish-gcse-eduqas-grammar-spine.html">← Back to the grammar spine</a></div>
  <div class="topbar">
    <div class="logo"><span class="spark">💭</span> Plans &amp; Opinions</div>
    <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div>
    <div class="score">⭐ <span id="score">0</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="spec">Eduqas GCSE Spanish (C820) · Grammar spine 4 of 10 — Plans and opinions</div>
  <div class="stage" id="stage">

    <!-- 0 WELCOME -->
    <section class="screen active">
      <span class="tag">Mini-Lesson</span>
      <h1>Plans &amp; opinions</h1>
      <p class="big">Once you can say what you <b>do</b>, you need to say what you're <b>going to do</b> and what you <b>think</b>. This lesson builds the tools of ambition and opinion: the <b>near future</b> (<span class="es">ir a</span> + infinitive), the <b>modal verbs</b>, the tricky <b>gustar</b> family, the polite <span class="es">me gustaría</span>, and the <b>opinion phrases</b> examiners love.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Voy a estudiar covers two English meanings">
          <rect x="18" y="45" width="170" height="52" rx="14" fill="#d9ebff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="103" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#0b5bbf">voy a estudiar</text>
          <text x="103" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="12" fill="#0b5bbf">ir a + infinitive</text>
          <line x1="188" y1="71" x2="360" y2="52" stroke="#12a67a" stroke-width="3"/>
          <line x1="188" y1="71" x2="360" y2="92" stroke="#12a67a" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="366" y="57" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">I'm going to study</text>
          <text x="366" y="97" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">I will study</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p>Work through each screen and answer the <b>questions</b> as you go — some ask you to <b>recognise</b> the right form, some ask you to <b>produce</b> it by typing. Collect ⭐ stars and press <b>Start</b>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 NEAR FUTURE INTRO -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">The near future · ir a + infinitive</span>
      <h2>Saying what you're going to do</h2>
      <p>The easiest way to talk about the future is exactly like English "going to": take the verb <span class="es">ir</span> (to go) + <span class="es">a</span> + an <b>infinitive</b>. It also translates plain "will".</p>
      <div class="eqn"><span class="es">ir</span> + a + infinitive<small>voy a estudiar = I'm going to study / I will study</small></div>
      <table class="conj" aria-label="ir in the present tense">
        <caption><span class="es">ir</span> — to go (recap)</caption>
        <tr><th>person</th><th>form</th></tr>
        <tr><td>yo</td><td>voy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tú</td><td>vas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>él / ella / usted</td><td>va</td></tr>
        <tr><td>nosotros</td><td>vamos</td></tr>
        <tr><td>vosotros</td><td>vais</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ellos / ustedes</td><td>van</td></tr>
      </table>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl v">Voy a estudiar</span> esta noche. — I <b>'m going to study</b> tonight.</li>
        <li><span class="hl v">Vamos a viajar</span> a España. — We <b>'re going to travel</b> to Spain.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Time markers</b> that signal the future: <span class="es">mañana</span> (tomorrow), <span class="es">la próxima semana</span> (next week), <span class="es">el año que viene</span> (next year), <span class="es">este fin de semana</span> (this weekend).</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 Q MCQ ir a + infinitive -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>What follows <span class="es">voy a</span>?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>You want to say <b>"I'm going to study"</b>. Choose: <b><span class="es">Voy a ____</span></b></span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q1" data-why="After 'ir a' you always use the infinitive — the unchanged '-ar/-er/-ir' form. So it's 'voy a estudiar', never 'voy a estudio' (that would be a conjugated present-tense form).">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1"><span class="es">estudiar</span> <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0"><span class="es">estudio</span> <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0"><span class="es">estudias</span> <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0"><span class="es">estudiando</span> <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 Q textans ir a production -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Type it</span>
      <h2>Your turn — the near future</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">1</span><span>Translate <b>"I'm going to eat"</b> into Spanish. <span style="color:var(--soft);font-size:.95rem">Three words.</span></span></div>
        <div class="textans" data-t="t1" data-answer="voy a comer">
          <input type="text" inputmode="text" maxlength="40" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" aria-label="Your answer">
          <button class="checkbtn">Check ✓</button>
        </div>
        <div class="hint">Hint: <span class="es">ir</span> in the "I" form (<span class="es">voy</span>) + <span class="es">a</span> + <span class="es">comer</span>.</div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-t1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 MODAL VERBS -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Modal verbs + infinitive</span>
      <h2>Can, want, must, have to</h2>
      <p>A <b>modal verb</b> is a helper verb followed by a <b>second verb in the infinitive</b>. You conjugate the modal; the second verb never changes:</p>
      <div class="trio">
        <table class="conj" aria-label="poder o to ue">
          <caption><span class="es">poder</span> — to be able / can</caption>
          <tr><td>p<span class="chg">ue</span>do</td></tr><tr><td>p<span class="chg">ue</span>des</td></tr><tr><td>p<span class="chg">ue</span>de</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:var(--soft)">podemos</td></tr><tr><td style="color:var(--soft)">podéis</td></tr><tr><td>p<span class="chg">ue</span>den</td></tr>
        </table>
        <table class="conj" aria-label="querer e to ie">
          <caption><span class="es">querer</span> — to want</caption>
          <tr><td>qu<span class="chg">ie</span>ro</td></tr><tr><td>qu<span class="chg">ie</span>res</td></tr><tr><td>qu<span class="chg">ie</span>re</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:var(--soft)">queremos</td></tr><tr><td style="color:var(--soft)">queréis</td></tr><tr><td>qu<span class="chg">ie</span>ren</td></tr>
        </table>
        <table class="conj" aria-label="deber regular">
          <caption><span class="es">deber</span> — must / should</caption>
          <tr><td>debo</td></tr><tr><td>debes</td></tr><tr><td>debe</td></tr>
          <tr><td>debemos</td></tr><tr><td>debéis</td></tr><tr><td>deben</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl v">Puedo</span> nadar. — I <b>can</b> swim. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(boot o→ue)</span></li>
        <li><span class="hl v">Quiero</span> salir. — I <b>want</b> to go out. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(boot e→ie)</span></li>
        <li><span class="hl v">Debes</span> estudiar. — You <b>should</b> study. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(regular)</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Three more musts:</b> <span class="es">tener que</span> + inf = "to have to" (<span class="es">tengo que estudiar</span>); <span class="es">hay que</span> + inf = impersonal "one must" (<span class="es">hay que practicar</span>); <span class="es">saber</span> + inf = "to know how to" (<span class="es">sé nadar</span> — I know how to swim; <span class="es">sé, sabes, sabe…</span>).</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 Q MCQ modal + infinitive -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>After a modal verb</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Complete: <b><span class="es">Tengo que ____</span></b> mañana. ("I have to work tomorrow.")</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q2" data-why="'Tener que' is a modal-style phrase meaning 'to have to' — the second verb stays in the infinitive. So 'tengo que trabajar', never the conjugated 'trabajo'.">
